{"type": "answer", "run_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-20261111", "scenario": "eval:retrieval", "source": "real", "setting": "small_lean", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 600, "k": 4, "rerank": false, "router": false}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q15", "request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-20261111-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q15", "start_time": "2026-11-11T04:38:51.763+00:00", "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-20261111-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q15", "question": "Why the ratio loss, and what are F, G and N?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "The ratio loss is introduced to prevent the model from collapsing to trivial solutions that either keep almost all vectors (eliminating speed gains) or over‑compress the sequence (losing essential information) by explicitly encouraging a desired compression ratio [S2]. It is inspired by load‑balancing losses used in Mixture‑of‑Experts models, which similarly need to keep expert usage balanced [S2]. The loss is defined in terms of three quantities: F, G and N [S4]. F denotes the fraction of input vectors that are actually selected (i.e., retained after routing) [S4]. G is the average boundary probability output by the routing module, providing a differentiable proxy for F [S4]. N controls the target compression ratio, acting as a hyper‑parameter that specifies how much the sequence should be compressed [S4].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0056", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b019"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0054", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b010", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b011", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b012", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b013", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b014", "dynamic_chunking